  "details": "When an avatar pedestal is destroy(), its thumbnail textures are not disposed of from VRAM. They will be disposed of from VRAM when the user leaves the instance.\n\nThis especially affects performance in worlds with a large number of pedestals, and some worlds have this issue:\n\nhttps://x.com/EngineerNeet/status/1968484682116272509 and their world is https://vrchat.com/home/world/wrld_02cb84c8-446a-484b-b52b-2c1777e55f8d.\n\nThis time, I tested two scenarios: loading all pedestals simultaneously and loading avatars one by one from a list every second onto a single pedestal.\n\n# Scenario 1: https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=JQZEfzWeNTA (5.0GiB -> 6.3GiB, 1.3GiB increased) \n\nIt Instantiate() pedestals for 500 avatars at the same time. Also Destroy() them does not free the thumbnail textures from VRAM.\n\nI also tried replacing the blueprint ID with an empty or any ID instead of Destroy() but no change in capacity was observed.\n\n# Scenario 2: https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=M-6EeTkcD4o (4.6GiB -> 4.9GiB, 0.3GiB increased) \n\nIt parses a text asset containing the IDs of 500 avatars and sets the avatar IDs in the list every second.\n\nIn fact, the Avatar Museum implements an avatar change system in a similar scenario: https://x.com/AvatarMuseum/status/1968651848077705567\n\nIt places two pedestals in the world and changes the avatar IDs of the pedestals when the \"Change Avatar\" button is interacted with.\n\nA related issue is that I do think the thumbnail texture resolution is too high, which causes a lot of VRAM to be used by it. afaik it's set to a resolution of 1200x900, which is too much for the avatar pedestal. Its resolution should be lower than 512x512, and the aspect ratio should be 1:1.\n\nThe test world I made can be accessed here: https://vrchat.com/home/world/wrld_735faf7d-c591-4484-a844-1f6b3275265e",
